---
id: weegloo-global-rules
type: rule
title: Weegloo MCP Global Rules
description: >
  These rules apply to all MCP resources and operations, including WebHosting, Content, and ContentType management.
  They are intended to ensure consistent behavior and proper use of MCP tools across all skills.
---

rules:
  - section: Confirm the Weegloo MCP is authenticated — at startup and on auth failures (hard gate)
    items:
      - **Before any Weegloo work, check the `mcp__weegloo__cma_*` management tools are actually present in this session** — first, before any code, scaffolding or config. Do **not** build a local app and only hit the blocker later.
      - **If they are missing the Weegloo MCP is not loaded — do not decide the task is impossible, and do not quietly build a local-only app instead.** It usually needs **sign-in (OAuth) and a restart**; seeing only **`mcp__weegloo-upload__CreateUpload`** is the typical symptom (a separate upload server, not the management tools). **Stop and tell the user, in their language,** that real resources can't be created yet, then ask whether to (a) set that up and continue, or (b) proceed local-only — making clear **no real Weegloo resources will be created**. Do **not** pick for them.
      - **On a mid-session auth failure (`401`, invalid/expired token, previously-working tools now rejected) do not guess a workaround or quietly stop** — say it is a credential problem, and fix it per MCP: **`weegloo`** uses **OAuth** → have the user re-authenticate (restart if the harness needs it); **`weegloo-upload`** holds a **token value in its config file** → ask the user for the token, pointing them at `https://console.weegloo.com/personal-access-token/connect?tool=weegloo-mcp`, and **edit the config file yourself**. Not every `403` is a credential problem.
      - **Report the Weegloo result first:** lead with what happened on Weegloo ("real resources: N created / 0 — reason"), not the local build. Never headline "Weegloo integration done" when nothing was created on Weegloo.

  - section: Select the working Organization and Space FIRST (hard gate)
    items:
      - **Before any Weegloo work the target `Organization` and `Space` must be decided.** Almost every resource lives **inside a specific Space**; the wrong one silently scatters the work into an arbitrary workspace with no error.
      - **On the user's FIRST Weegloo request of a session** — integrate / create / update / delete / upload / deploy, or anything targeting a `spaceId` — establish it **before** the first write or space-scoped read.
      - **Do not guess or auto-pick: never a default, never the first item of a list.** Ask the user to (a) use an **existing** Organization + Space or (b) **create new**. List theirs with the `/me/*-memberships` reads below, present names/codes, let them pick. To create: the Organization first (`cma_CreateOrganization`), then `cma_CreateSpace`, names confirmed.
      - **Reuse that `spaceId` for the rest of the work**; if a later request implies a **different** Space, stop and confirm the switch. Only exempt: read-only queries that are not space-scoped (own organizations/spaces, `GET /me`).

  - section: When unsure, read the docs first (do not guess)
    items:
      - If you are **not certain** how a Weegloo feature behaves — a query/filter parameter, `select`/`include` shape, locale rules, an error code, publish/version semantics, role scope — **look it up before acting**, and re-check after an unexpected error or empty result. Docs beat memory.
      - **Discover the doc path, never guess it:** take an **exact** path from **`https://docs.weegloo.com/llms.txt`** (or a link inside a page you already fetched) and fetch it with **`.md`**. HTML, a SPA shell or **403 / 404** means the **path is wrong** — re-derive it from `llms.txt`; do **not** retry variants or swap fetch tools. Procedure: **`weegloo-api-endpoints`**.

  - section: Read the right skill BEFORE architecture / ContentType design (hard gate)
    items:
      - These rule files are **safety-net summaries, not a substitute** for the skills. Having the rules in context does **not** satisfy this gate — invoke the skill.
      - **Router — `weegloo-platform-integration` FIRST** for any broad or vague "build / integrate / use Weegloo" request. **Naming Weegloo as the platform to build with IS the trigger — the words "integrate" / "connect" are NOT required, in English or ANY other language** — and a request naming **no** feature is the **strongest** case for the router, not an exemption. Do not skip it to start scaffolding a frontend.
      - **Service architecture** (which APIs / login / roles a product uses) → **`weegloo-service-architecture`** first. Do **not** decide CMA/CDA vs ACMA/ACDA, token type or role scope from memory or these summaries.
      - **End-user sign-in / sign-up** — a `ServiceLogin`, a `ServiceUserRole`, or scoping ACMA/ACDA member access (`createdBy :self`, `roleOverride`, `defaultRole`) → **`weegloo-service-login`** first, then **`weegloo-service-login-client`** before wiring the OAuth flow; a **native Android / iOS** app also needs its callback deep link in **`ServiceLogin.allowedCallbackUrls`** before the first sign-in attempt. `weegloo-service-architecture` / `weegloo-space-role` only **point to** these skills — reading them, or the SDK skill alone, does not satisfy this gate.
      - **ContentType design** (fields, `localized`, ShortText/LongText/RichText, validations, `publishWithAuthor`, `Refer`) → **`weegloo-create-content-type`** first, plus **`weegloo-default-locale`** whenever any field is multi-locale. **`ShortText` stops at 64 characters**, so **a length you cannot verify or predict is never a `ShortText`** however id-like its name (a PG session id, a token, a URL) — then **searched or queried on → `LongText`, never → `RichText`**.
      - **WebHosting deploy** → **`weegloo-delivery-access-token`** before provisioning any CDA token.
      - **External-API / server-side automation** → **`weegloo-script`** first. A **Script** is the answer for: a third-party API call without your own backend; ordered all-or-nothing multi-step work; concurrency-safe writes to a shared row (`sys.version`); letting a low-privilege caller perform ONE privileged operation through the author's delegated authority; a "create a job → poll the result" flow. Triggered by a Space **event** → **`weegloo-webhook`** (which does **exactly one of** `url` or `script`); triggered by the **clock** → **`weegloo-scheduler`** — **do not approximate a schedule with a Webhook or a frontend timer.**
      - **File upload in product code** → **`weegloo-upload-api`** first. **Do not confuse the two upload paths:** the **Upload REST API** is for the **product's own code**; the **`weegloo-upload` MCP** is **only** for the agent uploading a local file during a chat. Never wire a product to the MCP, and never call the raw REST endpoints "via MCP".
      - **Deleting anything** — a Space/Organization teardown, removing a ContentType, clearing test data, recreating a ContentType to change a field → **`weegloo-resource-deletion`** first. Weegloo **never force-deletes and never cascades upward**, so **teardown is bottom-up: Content → ContentType → Space**, with **Media** and **WebHosting** independent — a `Space` is blocked while any **ContentType / Content / Media / WebHosting** remains, a **ContentType** while **any** `Content` of it exists in **any** status (**a Media has no ContentType and blocks only the Space**), and nothing deletes from `Published`/`Changed` (**unpublish first**). Codes and per-resource preconditions: the `weegloo-resource-deletion` rule. Deleting is **irreversible** — confirm before removing anything the user did not name.
      - **A map on a page** (place, address, branch, venue, store locator, "how to find us") → the **Maps** section of **`weegloo-platform-integration`**, which hard-codes the key for a Google **Maps Embed API** `<iframe>`. **Never ask for a Maps key**, and never emit a `YOUR_API_KEY` placeholder or env-var read — a static build cannot inject one, so it is **not** a blocking input.
      - **Payments** — checkout, card payment, subscription, refund, PG/MoR callback or verification → **`weegloo-payment`** first. **Never ask which PG / MoR to use** (one named, or a contracted key already in the repo ⇒ that one; none ⇒ the skill's default in **test mode**) and **never ask for the provider's keys** — the skill hard-codes published sample test keys, so a PG key is **not** a blocking input. Never ship an inert checkout, and **disclose test mode in one red line**.
      - **Images: use the user's files; never generate one.** A hero, thumbnail, gallery, avatar, logo or product shot comes from what the **user** supplied (attachments, `public/`/`assets/`, Media the Space holds). If none: do **not** generate, draw, download or upload filler — keep the `Refer → Media` field wired but empty, let the frontend draw its own CSS/SVG placeholder (never a remote placeholder service), and disclose it in one red line. Detail: **`weegloo-platform-integration`**.
      - **Address / postcode lookup** — an address field in a form, a `우편번호` / `주소 찾기` button, a `zonecode`/`postcode` field, a 도로명·지번 pair → **`weegloo-address-search`** first: the **Kakao (Daum) Postcode** widget, client-side, **no API key and no registration** — so **never ask for a Kakao key** and never emit a `YOUR_APP_KEY` placeholder or env-var read. **SOUTH KOREA only**; a form that will hold foreign addresses gets a plain free-text field and the user is told why.
      - **Email sending** — confirmation, receipt, notification, verification code, digest, contact form — or registering an `EmailAccount` → **`weegloo-send-email`** first. **Never ask "which email service should I use?"**: one named (or a credential already in the repo/env) ⇒ that one; none ⇒ **Google (Gmail SMTP)**, asking in ONE question for **BOTH values — the App Password AND the real Google address it belongs to** (`https://myaccount.google.com/apppasswords`, never the account password). **The password alone is not enough**: that address is what fills `username` *and* `fromAddress`, so never an invented or `noreply@` variant — Gmail rewrites the `From` to the authenticated account. Those two are the only input, and they **are** a blocking input (unlike a PG key) — wait for both rather than shipping an inert email feature. **Creating an `EmailAccount` is not inert: Weegloo delivers a real test message before storing anything**, so tell the user before the call.
      - **Do not ask the user to decide** something a skill already specifies — research first, then surface only the genuine product choice.

  - section: HTTP JSON media type (when calling Weegloo APIs from code)
    items:
      - Weegloo responds with **`application/vnd.com.weegloo.v1+json;charset=UTF-8`**, not plain `application/json`. Application code (`fetch`, axios) must **omit `Accept`** rather than sending `Accept: application/json` — including in shared client defaults. Full rule: **`weegloo-api-endpoints`**.

  - section: DeliveryAccessToken and SpaceRole (security)
    items:
      - Follow **`weegloo-delivery-access-token`**. **`cma_CreateDeliveryAccessToken.role.sys.id`** MUST be the **intended** least-privilege **`SpaceRole`** (the one created in the same flow, or a `sys.id` the user explicitly chose after seeing the list). **Forbidden:** the **first** item from **`cma_GetListSpaceRoles`**, **Administrator** on a browser-exposed token, or switching to Administrator after **`WGL422001`** or any other error.
      - **`allowedReferrers` (DeliveryAccessToken / SpaceAccessToken) is off by default — do not set it unless the user explicitly asks.** It pins a token to its current origins, so a later origin change (custom domain, changed subdomain) refuses every call while the token still looks valid. When updating a token for any other reason, **resend the existing list** — an update replaces the whole field and would otherwise clear it.

  - section: SpaceAccessToken (security — Space-scoped read/write token; hard gate)
    items:
      - A **`SpaceAccessToken`** (`SPCAT…`, `cma_CreateSpaceAccessToken`) **reads AND writes** inside **one Space**, its power set entirely by a **bound `SpaceRole`**; it never reaches the Organization, the account plane, or ACMA/ACDA. Full model: **`weegloo-space-access-token`**.
      - **Bound role = the blast radius; scope it to the exposure.** A token exposed publicly (browser / anonymous posting) must carry a role narrow enough that a leak is acceptable — e.g. create-only into one `ContentType`. **Forbidden:** the first item from `cma_GetListSpaceRoles`, Administrator or a broad write role on a publicly-exposed token, or widening the role after an error.
      - **Anti-escalation — do NOT work around `WGL422001`** (the caller can only bind a role they already hold, or own as Space admin). **Never** fall back to Administrator or a broader role; explain and offer legitimate paths.
      - **Space *settings* are token-gated, not role-gated — a `SpaceAccessToken` reaches NONE of them.** Every `SETTING_*` action is **403 for a SpaceAccessToken regardless of the bound role**. Do **not** "fix" such a 403 by adding the settings action to the bound role — it changes nothing; that surface needs a **console session or a PAT**. The bound role governs only the data plane (Content / ContentType / Media + publish/archive, and Script `Execute`). **Consequence:** a SpaceAccessToken cannot bootstrap a Space (no Locale, Webhook, Scheduler, ServiceLogin, WebHosting, or token issuance) — provision with a console session / PAT first, then hand the SAT to the runtime.

  - section: Plan limit / quota exceeded — explain and guide to upgrade
    items:
      - **Applies to ANY plan-limit / quota error** (a **`WGL429*`** code or an HTTP **429**-class "limit/quota exceeded", for any resource) — the current plan's limit has been reached, not a transient failure. **Do NOT silently retry, loop, or just surface the raw code.**
      - Answer in the **user's language** with what failed (naming the code), the upgrade path — **https://docs.weegloo.com/pricing/pricing/** — and alternatives (free a slot by deleting a resource of the same type; wait if it is genuinely a rate limit). **Then ASK what they want to do:** never auto-upgrade, auto-delete, or retry in a loop — this is a user/billing decision.
      - **State the current plan ONLY if verifiable — never guess a tier.** With the Usage/Limits tools (MCP installed with `?group=extra` / `?group=all`) you may state the concrete plan and usage; without them say only that the current plan's limit has been reached, naming no tier.
      - **Never engineer around the cap:** do not switch `Organization`/`Space` to dodge it, and do not delete resources without explicit user confirmation.

  - section: Highlight what the user must act on or must know (green / red where the surface supports it)
    items:
      - "**Two kinds of line get colour, in a `diff` fenced block — the ONLY colouring mechanism.** **Green (`+ `) = values the user must act on** (the live site URL, an OAuth Redirect URI to register, a CNAME target). **Red (`- `) = facts they MUST know about what shipped** (payments on test keys and nothing actually charged, placeholder images). Buried in prose, an act-on value is skimmed past and a must-know fact is missed entirely, which is far worse. Never put a value the user must copy on a red line — red reads as *removed* — and never soften a must-know limitation into green."
      - "**Keep the two in separate blocks, one to three lines each** (colouring everything is identical to colouring nothing), and put the red block with the disclosure it belongs to, not after the sign-off. **The `+ ` / `- ` is a rendering marker, not part of the content:** where a value must be pasted exactly, say so — a pasted `+` produces a mismatch error with no clue why. **Never emit raw ANSI escape codes** (they print as garbage or ride invisibly into what the user copies), and treat colour as an accent: everything it conveys must also be stated in words."

  - section: MCP Tool Usage
    items:
      - Always use the designated MCP tools to create, read, update or delete resources. Direct API calls or manual edits outside them — Swagger, raw HTTP, or a deploy script such as `scripts/deploy-weegloo.mjs` that calls Weegloo APIs — are **prohibited for the agent**. (Human developers doing local development may call the APIs directly.)
      - **Exceptions (only these two):** the read-only, unauthenticated GETs **`GET https://cma.weegloo.com/v1/policy/terms`** (`weegloo-terms-consent`) and **`GET https://ai.weegloo.com/v1/version`** (`weegloo-version`) — neither has an MCP tool. Nothing else may be called over raw HTTP by the agent.

  - section: Current User Resources (Me)
    items:
      - "For the current user's own resources use **`GET /me/organization-memberships`** and **`GET /me/space-memberships`** (NOT `/organizations`), with `include=1` to resolve names/codes."
      - "**Response shape — the related resource is nested under `sys`, NOT at the item root.** The Space id is at **`item.sys.space.sys.id`** (and on an OrganizationMembership, `item.sys.organization.sys.id`). Reading the flat `item.space.sys.id` yields **`undefined`** — the common membership-gate bug where every real member is wrongly rejected. Snippets: **`weegloo-user-login`**."

  - section: ACMA Content paths are nested under ContentType (no flat /contents)
    items:
      - "**On ACMA every Content operation is addressed UNDER its ContentType** — `/v1/spaces/{spaceId}/content-types/{contentTypeId}/contents[/{contentId}]`; the flat `/contents` paths **do not exist there** (404). Holding only a `contentId`, resolve its ContentType rather than falling back to a flat path (**ACDA** has both forms). Detail: **`weegloo-api-endpoints`**."

  - section: Resource Search
    items:
      - List endpoints share `limit` (max **100**), `skip` (max **100**), `order`, `include`, `select`, `filter` — shapes, defaults and operators are canonical at **https://docs.weegloo.com/api/reference/common/query-parameters**; read it rather than guessing. More rows come from cursor paging, never a bigger number.
      - "**References are NEVER embedded.** A Content does **not** carry its Media (or any linked Content / User) inline — a reference field holds only a stub `{ sys: { id, type: \"Refer\", targetType: \"Media\" } }`. Resolve it by fetching that `sys.id`, or with **`?include=1`** and reading the response's **singular `include` object keyed by PascalCase `targetType`** (`include.Media`, `include.Content`, …). Do **not** assume `fields.image` / `fields.file` / an author already holds the URL or the linked document. Detail: **`weegloo-api-query-optimization`**."

  - section: Projection — `select` is the default on every read, not an optimization
    items:
      - "**Every read of Content or Media names the fields it needs** — `select=sys.id,fields.title`, on **every** list call. Not a tuning tip for when something feels slow; it is how a read is written, and an unprojected read is the exception that needs a reason."
      - "**This binds YOUR OWN calls, not just code you generate.** `select` is a parameter on the `mcp__weegloo__cma_GetList*` tools. A bare `GetListContents` returns whole documents — every locale bucket of every field of every row — into your context window, where it displaces the actual task. Nothing truncates it for you."
      - "**One `select` per request, paths comma-separated** (`order` likewise) — do not repeat the key. Recipes, include/exclude modes, keeping `order` keys projected and `sys.id[in]` batching: **`weegloo-api-query-optimization`**."

  - section: Searching `fields.*` — Advanced Search is required
    items:
      - "**Content lives in `fields.*`, and filtering there is EXACT match unless Advanced Search is on.** Any filter or `order` touching a `fields.*` path needs the header **`X-Weegloo-Advanced-Search: true`**; with it, `eq` on a full-text-enabled **LongText** matches rows that *contain* the term. Without it a substring query returns an **empty list rather than an error** — which is exactly why this gets missed."
      - "**Send the header on every `fields.*` filter or `order`, not only when you want substring matching — it is also what keeps the query FAST.** Without it the request is served from a store indexed on the **system axes only** (`sys.*` — Space, ContentType, owner, status, tags, references, recency — plus `createdBy`); there is **no index for `fields.*`**, so that part degrades into a scan that runs very slow and then **times out** — scaling with the Space content, so it passes against seed data and fails in production. When you must filter `fields.*` without the header, **pair it with the system axes** so the unindexed part runs over a small subset."
      - "**Do not read an empty result as \"no matches\" and fall back to filtering in memory.** Filtering the loaded array is correct only when that array is already the whole dataset; over a paged or unknown-size list it searches the rows you happen to hold and silently misses the rest — search server-side via the list API + `links.next` instead."
      - "**Two things the filter key itself needs** (both silently return nothing when wrong): the **locale segment** — `fields.title.en-US[eq]=…`, required for **every** field including non-localized ones — and, on the flat `/contents` list, the **ContentType scope** `sys.contentType.sys.id=<id>` (a bare `contentType=<id>` is not enough). Neither applies to `sys.*` filters."
      - "**The header is an HTTP-only lever — the MCP tools cannot send it.** A `fields.*` filter issued over MCP is **exact-match only and unindexed**, which on a Space with real content is where your own tool call hangs or times out. **Prefer `sys.*` filters for your own MCP list calls**, and scope a `fields.*` one by ContentType first. Real text search is application code calling CMA/CDA over HTTP with the header — do **not** conclude from an empty MCP result that the data is absent."
      - "**RichText and Json fields are not searchable at all** — searchability is decided when the field is modelled (`weegloo-create-content-type`), not bolted on afterwards."

  - section: Pagination Handling
    items:
      - Page with the response's **`links.next`** / **`links.prev`** — call that path, or pass its `next` cursor as the `next` param; `links.next` absent ⇒ no more pages. Mechanics: **`weegloo-list-pagination`**.
      - "**A list you did not page is not the dataset** — the default page is 15 rows and the cap is 100, so treating one page as the complete set (counting it, searching it, rendering it as everything) is silently wrong."

  - section: Resource Updates
    items:
      - "**Changing PART of a Content / ContentType / Media → `PATCH` (`cma_PatchOne*`), not `PUT`** — send only the paths that change. `PUT` / `cma_UpdateOne*` replaces the WHOLE document, so reach for it only when that is what you mean."
      - "**Updates are FULL REPLACEMENT: resend ALL fields, not just the changed ones** — anything omitted is wiped, with no error. On a version conflict, GET the resource for the current `sys.version` and retry with that fresh version."
      - When updating a **versioned** resource — **Content**, **Media**, **ContentType** — include the `x-weegloo-version` header with the current `sys.version`. Not every resource is versioned (a **Scheduler** has none and takes no version header), so check the resource's own skill rather than assuming.
      - "**MCP update tools take the version via the parameter named `X-Weegloo-Version`** (exactly as capitalized) — NOT `version` or `xWeeglooVersion`."

  - section: WebHosting platform limits
    items:
      - "**Weegloo WebHosting only** (not apps self-hosted elsewhere): static export, `index.html` at the ZIP root, ≤ **300** archive entries — **`weegloo-web-hosting-rules`**."
      - "**Weegloo REST from a WebHosted site must run in the BROWSER** (`fetch` / AJAX to CDA): there is no server on Weegloo to do it per request, so SSR or a server-side client builds and deploys cleanly and then never runs. Elsewhere-hosted apps may use SSR as they choose."

  - section: Filter Parameters (API List Endpoints)
    items:
      - Filters are direct query params (NOT wrapped in `filter[]`), e.g. `name[prefix]=value`; the operator set and per-field-type support are in the same query-parameters reference.
      - "**Tripwire — Refer fields *are* filterable by target id:** `fields.<refer>.<locale>.sys.id[eq]=<id>` (`[in]`, `[exists]` and `sys.targetType` too). The locale segment is required here as well, and **omitting it does not fail quietly** — it errors blaming the **field type** while the **path** is what is malformed. Do not read that as \"Refer isn't filterable\" and fall back to id strings or client-side grouping."
      - "**Tripwire — Advanced Search inside a Script** is the per-statement flag **`advanced` on `ResourceFind` / `ResourceForEach` / `ResourceCount`, defaulting to `true`. Leave it there.** `advanced: false` has **no index for `fields.*`**, so a `where`/`order` over your own content fields degrades into a scan that times out — passing on test data, failing in production. Its one legitimate use is seeing a just-written row (the indexed path catches up ~1s later, so a `ResourceFind` returns `null` or a `ResourceCount` comes back one short); even then prefer reading **by id** with `ResourceRead`, which is never indexed. Detail: **`weegloo-script`**."
